      "id": 1,
      "text": "Northfield became an independent town in 1780 after residents petitioned the state because traveling to Canterbury’s center was too far.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northfield%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "The Boston, Concord and Montreal Railroad opened in 1848, linking Northfield to Tilton and sparking a mill‑town boom.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        4
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Northfield%2C_New_Hampshire",
      "category": "transport",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "Northfield Town Hall, originally a 1828 church, now sits on the National Register at 21 Summer St., overlooking the cemetery.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        6
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nhmagazine.com/navigating-northfield/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "The Tilton Memorial Arch, a 55‑foot granite monument finished in 1886, actually stands in Northfield despite its name.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        16
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nhmagazine.com/navigating-northfield/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "Hall Memorial Library on Park Street, a historic community hub, opened in the early 20th century and still serves locals.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        14
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 2"
      ],
      "source": "https://www.nhmagazine.com/navigating-northfield/",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "The old Boston, Concord and Montreal depot, surrounded by vintage passenger and freight cars, remains on an active Merrimack Valley line used mainly for tourist trains.",
